(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #109, owner ruling 2026-08-29: "Freeing a list should
 free every item within the list as well." `_free_list` walks its own
 slots by their per-element type tag (list.asm's LIST_TAG_LIST=4,
 LIST_TAG_MAP=5) and recurses into `_free_list`/`_free_map` for each -
 both are unconditionally heap blocks, so this is safe without knowing
 anything about what's inside them. Nested collections are pointer-in on
 0.4.14 (probed separately, see REPORT-109.md "Nested collections: copy-in
 or pointer-in?"), so `inner`/`m` below are the SAME blocks `outer` frees.
 This only proves the outer Free does not crash and the outer list ends up
 empty; it does not - and cannot - prove the freed pages were unmapped from
 inside the language, since reading `inner`/`m` directly afterwards is
 exactly the dangling-alias hazard flagged in the report as a question for
 the master, not something this test exercises.)

a list called inner is [1, 2, 3].
a map called m is {"a": 1, "b": 2}.
a list called outer is [inner, m, "x", 42].
print outer's length.
Free outer.
print outer's length.
print outer's empty.
print outer.
